  <h2>Before you start</h2>
  <p>
    Work on a new branch with a clean working tree, so the migration is one
    diff you can review or throw away:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code="git switch -c migrate-to-blume" lang="bash" />
  <p>
    Then save the URLs your site serves today. MkDocs writes a{" "}
    <code>sitemap.xml</code> from your <code>site_url</code>, so pull the paths
    out of the live one and keep the list outside the repository:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={saveUrls} lang="bash" />
  <p>
    Replace <code>docs.acme.example</code> with your domain in both places.
    MkDocs URLs end in a slash and Blume's don't, so the last expression drops
    it. The sitemap leaves out the pages the redirects plugin writes, so add
    each <code>redirect_maps</code> key as a URL. In this guide's project,{" "}
    <code>setup.md</code> is <code>/setup</code>:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code="echo /setup >> ../old-urls.txt" lang="bash" />
  <p>
    Keep the Python setup until you switch: the agent builds the old site once
    more to compare heading anchors. You also need Node.js 22.19 or later, and
    Claude Code or Codex installed and signed in.
  </p>

  <h2>Run the migration</h2>
  <p>From the folder that holds <code>mkdocs.yml</code>, run:</p>
  <CodeBlock code="npx blume migrate mkdocs --claude" lang="bash" />
  <p>
    To use Codex, swap <code>--claude</code> for <code>--codex</code>. On pnpm
    12, run it with <code>pnpm dlx --allow-build=esbuild</code> instead of{" "}
    <code>npx</code>. Leave out <code>mkdocs</code> and Blume detects the source
    from <code>mkdocs.yml</code>, <code>mkdocs.yaml</code>,{" "}
    <code>zensical.toml</code>, or <code>properdocs.yml</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>blume migrate</code> converts nothing itself. It opens the agent on the{" "}
    <code>blume-migrate</code> skill inside the package, pointed at its MkDocs
    reference. Following it, the agent:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Runs the bundled MkDocs codemod over <code>docs/</code>. It converts
      admonitions, content tabs, snippets, code block options, heading ids,
      icons, and keys, moves each page's H1 into its <code>title</code>, renames
      only the pages that now need MDX to <code>.mdx</code>, points links at the
      renamed files, and reports everything it leaves.
    </li>
    <li>
      Works through that report: dropped buttons and image sizes, unknown icons,
      mkdocstrings and macros, and anything MDX would reject.
    </li>
    <li>
      Writes <code>blume.config.ts</code> from <code>mkdocs.yml</code>: title,
      logo, colors, edit links, social links, analytics, header tabs, and
      redirects.
    </li>
    <li>Rebuilds <code>nav</code> as folders and <code>meta.ts</code> files.</li>
    <li>
      Pins the old heading ids with the bundled <code>pin-heading-ids.mjs</code>,
      comparing your MkDocs build with Blume's.
    </li>
    <li>
      Adds a <code>package.json</code>, updates CI and any repository files that
      named the renamed pages, and runs <code>blume build</code> and{" "}
      <code>blume validate --strict</code> until they pass.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Its report has one block per page. Part of it, for the example project
    below:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={codemodReport} lang="txt" />
  <Callout type="info">
    The codemod is deterministic, but the rest of the agent's work is judgment,
    so two runs on one repository can differ. Read the diff rather than
    trusting it. It ends with a summary of what it migrated, dropped, and
    approximated.
  </Callout>

  <h2>The example project</h2>
  <p>
    The rest of this guide moves this Material site: an explicit{" "}
    <code>nav</code> with a nested section, tabs, admonitions, content tabs, a
    snippet, and a redirect.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={mkdocsYml} lang="yaml" title="mkdocs.yml" />
  <p>Its files before the migration:</p>
  <CodeBlock code={filesBefore} lang="text" />
  <p>And after:</p>
  <CodeBlock code={filesAfter} lang="text" />

  <h2>Write the Blume config</h2>
  <p>
    Map only what <code>mkdocs.yml</code> sets, since every Blume option has a
    default:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={blumeConfig} lang="ts" title="blume.config.ts" />
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>site_name</code> and <code>site_description</code> become{" "}
      <code>title</code> and <code>description</code>. A{" "}
      <code>docs_dir</code> other than <code>docs</code> becomes{" "}
      <code>content.root</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>site_url</code> becomes <code>deployment.site</code>. Blume detects
      it on Vercel and Netlify; anywhere else, keep it, or the sitemap and
      social cards go missing. A path in it, like <code>/uv/</code>, becomes{" "}
      <code>deployment.base</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>repo_url</code> and <code>edit_uri</code> become <code>github</code>,
      for edit links on <code>main</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Move the logo from <code>docs/assets/</code> to <code>public/</code>. A
      favicon named <code>favicon.png</code> (or <code>.svg</code>,{" "}
      <code>.ico</code>) in <code>public/</code> is picked up with no config.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>primary: teal</code> becomes <code>theme.accent</code>, a preset
      or any CSS color. Blume follows the system color scheme and always shows
      a toggle. This project's <code>extra.css</code> only set a Material
      variable, so it goes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>copyright</code> becomes{" "}
      <a href="/docs/configuration#footer"><code>footer.copyright</code></a>,
      which is plain text, so <code>&amp;copy;</code> turns into{" "}
      <code>©</code>. Links in <code>extra.social</code> would move to{" "}
      <code>footer.socials</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If <code>mkdocs.yml</code> sits at the root of a Python or Rust repository,
    put <code>blume.config.ts</code> and <code>package.json</code> in{" "}
    <code>docs/</code> instead, with <code>content.root: "."</code>.{" "}
    <code>blume build</code> empties the project's <code>dist/</code>, which is
    where Python and Rust tools write their packages.
  </p>

  <h2>Rebuild the navigation</h2>
  <p>
    Blume builds the sidebar from folders, not a <code>nav</code> list.
    Folders become groups, files become pages labeled by their{" "}
    <code>title</code>, and pages sort <code>index</code> first, then by
    numeric filename prefix, then alphabetically. The codemod already turned
    each <code>nav</code> title that differs from a page's heading, like{" "}
    <code>Deploy: guides/deploy.md</code>, into <code>sidebar.label</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    A section title or order the folder doesn't give you goes in a{" "}
    <code>meta.ts</code>. Without one, <code>getting-started</code> would read
    "Getting Started":
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={gettingStartedMeta} lang="ts" title="docs/getting-started/meta.ts" />
  <p>
    A nested section with no folder, like Advanced, would flatten into
    Guides. Move its pages into a{" "}
    <a href="/docs/content#group-folders">group folder</a>, a folder name in
    parentheses: the sidebar nests them under "Advanced", and their URLs don't
    change, so they need no redirects:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={webhooksAfter} lang="md" title="docs/guides/(advanced)/webhooks.md" />
  <p>
    The moved page sits a folder deeper, so its link to{" "}
    <code>../reference/cli.md</code> becomes <code>../../reference/cli.md</code>,
    and links to it should name its new path. Material renders second-level
    sections as collapsible, so give the folder <code>display: "group"</code>:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={advancedMeta} lang="ts" title="docs/guides/(advanced)/meta.ts" />
  <p>
    A section that <code>nav</code> places under another folder, like uv's{" "}
    <code>pip/</code> pages listed under Concepts, can't use a group folder:
    moving the files changes their URL segment. Move them anyway and set each
    page's <code>slug</code> to its old URL.
  </p>
  <p>Also check:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      A folder's <code>index</code> page links its group's header and also
      lists as the group's first row. To show it once, as Material does, set{" "}
      <code>sidebar: {"{ hidden: true }"}</code> on the index: only the row
      goes, and the page stays in <code>sitemap.xml</code> and search.
    </li>
    <li>
      MkDocs keeps a numeric prefix like <code>01-</code> in the URL. Blume
      reads it as sort order and drops it, so add a redirect, or set a{" "}
      <code>slug</code> that keeps the old path.
    </li>
    <li>
      Blume lists pages you left out of <code>nav</code>. Set{" "}
      <code>sidebar: {"{ hidden: true }"}</code> on any that should stay out.
    </li>
    <li>
      Each tab in <code>navigation.tabs</code> scopes the sidebar to its
      folder, like Material's tabs.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Convert the pages</h2>
  <p>Here's the install page before:</p>
  <CodeBlock code={installBefore} lang="md" title="docs/getting-started/install.md" />
  <p>
    And after the codemod. Content tabs that each hold one code block become a{" "}
    <code>&lt;CodeGroup&gt;</code>, the admonition becomes a directive, the
    heading's id is pinned, and the snippet becomes an include. Includes must
    sit inside <code>docs/</code>, so the codemod copied{" "}
    <code>includes/support.md</code> to <code>docs/_snippets/</code>, where the
    underscore keeps it from becoming a page, and converted its admonition too:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={installAfter} lang="mdx" title="docs/getting-started/install.mdx" />
  <p>The quickstart before:</p>
  <CodeBlock code={quickstartBefore} lang="md" title="docs/getting-started/quickstart.md" />
  <p>And after. Callouts don't collapse, so the tip loses its color:</p>
  <CodeBlock code={quickstartAfter} lang="mdx" title="docs/getting-started/quickstart.mdx" />
  <p>
    The codemod reports what it can't decide. Here, it dropped the home page's{" "}
    <code>{"{ .md-button }"}</code> attributes, so the buttons become cards by
    hand, and <code>hide</code> became <code>mode: center</code>:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={indexAfter} lang="mdx" title="docs/index.mdx" />
  <p>
    Pages with no extension syntax, like <code>cli.md</code>, stay{" "}
    <code>.md</code> and only trade their heading for a <code>title</code>:
    MDX treats <code>{"{"}</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, and HTML comments as
    syntax, so the codemod renames only the pages that need it.
  </p>
  <p>
    To list any MkDocs syntax left in the content, run this until it prints
    only what you mean to keep:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={leftovers} lang="bash" />

  <h2>Replace the plugins</h2>
  <p>Take each entry under <code>plugins</code> in turn:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>search</strong> is built in, so delete it.{" "}
      <code>search.exclude</code> and <code>search.boost</code> keep working.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>redirects</strong> becomes <code>redirects</code>, one per{" "}
      <code>redirect_maps</code> entry, written as URLs.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>blog</strong> posts become <a href="/docs/advanced/blog">pages
      with <code>type: blog</code></a> and a flat <code>date</code> (a nested{" "}
      <code>date.created</code> fails the build). Blume
      generates no index, archive, or category pages, so write a{" "}
      <code>blog/index.mdx</code>, drop <code>categories</code>, and redirect
      dated URLs like <code>/blog/2024/01/31/hello/</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>tags</strong> becomes <code>search.tags</code>, a facet on
      hosted search providers, with no tag index pages.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>social</strong> cards are built in as{" "}
      <a href="/docs/discoverability/open-graph">Open Graph images</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>git-revision-date-localized</strong> becomes{" "}
      <code>lastModified: "git"</code>. Git committers have no equivalent.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>llmstxt</strong> is built in: <code>/llms.txt</code> and a{" "}
      <code>.md</code> copy of every page. Redirect the plugin's{" "}
      <code>/page/index.md</code> URLs to <code>/page.md</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>macros</strong>: plain values from <code>extra</code> move to{" "}
      <a href="/docs/content/variables"><code>variables</code></a>, which use the
      same <code>{"{{ name }}"}</code> syntax (names can't contain dots). Jinja
      logic, filters, and macro functions have no equivalent.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>awesome-pages</strong> <code>.pages</code> files become{" "}
      <code>meta.ts</code> files, and <strong>glightbox</strong> goes, since
      images zoom on click by default.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>mike</strong> versions move to{" "}
      <a href="/guides/version-markdown-documentation">Blume's versioning</a>,
      and <strong>i18n</strong> to <a href="/docs/content/i18n">i18n</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>mkdocstrings</strong> has no replacement. Keep publishing that
      reference separately and link to it. For an HTTP API, an OpenAPI spec can
      replace it: see{" "}
      <a href="/guides/fastapi-documentation-website">the FastAPI guide</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Keep heading links working</h2>
  <p>
    MkDocs and Blume turn headings into ids differently: MkDocs numbers a
    repeated heading <code>example_1</code> where Blume writes{" "}
    <code>example-1</code>, strips accents Blume keeps, and collapses
    punctuation Blume doesn't. Links from other sites to those headings land at
    the top of the page. After a <code>blume build</code>, the agent builds the
    old site and runs the bundled script, which pins the old id on each heading
    that changed:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code="node node_modules/blume/skills/blume-migrate/scripts/pin-heading-ids.mjs --old ../old-site --write"
    lang="bash"
  />
  <p>
    It rebuilds and runs it again until it reports nothing left to pin. A
    pinned heading reads <code>## Basic usage [#basic-usage_1]</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Check every old URL</h2>
  <p>
    Start <code>npx blume dev</code>, then walk the list you saved and print
    every old URL that doesn't reach a page:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={checkUrls} lang="bash" />
  <p>
    Redirects count as passing, since <code>-L</code> follows them. Add a
    redirect for each path it prints, and rerun it until it prints nothing.
    Here, the only one is <code>/setup</code>, already in the config: the moved
    webhooks page kept its URL in its group folder. After you deploy,
    run it once more with your site's URL in place of{" "}
    <code>http://localhost:4321</code>. For status codes, patterns, and what
    each host does with them, see{" "}
    <a href="/guides/documentation-url-migration-redirects">Move documentation URLs while preserving old links</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Links around the web still carry MkDocs' slash. <code>blume dev</code> and{" "}
    <code>blume preview</code> redirect a slashed URL to the page, as the{" "}
    <code>vercel()</code> adapter does, and a static build serves it from the
    page's folder on other hosts, so the list doesn't need both spellings.
  </p>

  <h2>Update the rest of the repository</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Search the repository for the paths of pages that became{" "}
      <code>.mdx</code>: release tooling that bumps versions inside pages, bot
      configs, CI path filters, and ignore files often name them.
    </li>
    <li>
      If you format with Prettier, extend any <code>*.md</code> overrides in{" "}
      <code>.prettierrc</code> and <code>.editorconfig</code> to{" "}
      <code>*.mdx</code> before you reformat:
    </li>
  </ul>
  <CodeBlock code={prettierrc} lang="json" title=".prettierrc" />
  <ul>
    <li>
      Replace the <code>mkdocs build</code> or <code>mkdocs gh-deploy</code>{" "}
      step in CI with Node.js, an install, <code>npx blume build</code>, and{" "}
      <code>npx blume validate --strict</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Remove the Python docs dependencies once you've switched, and regenerate
      the Python lockfile in the same change.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Build and validate</h2>
  <p>Stop the dev server, then build the site and check every link in it:</p>
  <CodeBlock code={"npx blume build\nnpx blume validate --strict"} lang="bash" />
  <p>
    <code>blume build</code> fails on invalid frontmatter, pages that don't
    compile, and bad config. <code>blume validate --strict</code> checks every
    link, anchor, and asset, so a relative link you missed in a moved page
    shows up as <code>BLUME_BROKEN_LINK</code>. Fix what they report rather
    than passing <code>--no-strict</code>, which drops the pages that fail.
  </p>

  <h2>Deploy and switch over</h2>
  <p>
    Many MkDocs sites publish to GitHub Pages with <code>mkdocs gh-deploy</code>,
    and a Blume site can stay there.{" "}
    <a href="/guides/markdown-docs-github-pages">Deploy Markdown docs to GitHub Pages</a>{" "}
    has the workflow. A <code>site_url</code> with a path, like a project site,
    splits into a <code>site</code> and a <code>base</code>:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`deployment: { site: "https://acme.github.io", base: "/widget-sdk" },`}
    lang="ts"
    title="blume.config.ts"
  />
  <p>
    GitHub Pages serves Blume's redirect pages for exact redirects and
    ignores patterns. For other hosts, see{" "}
    <a href="/docs/deployment#redirects">Redirects</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your last MkDocs build stays on the <code>gh-pages</code> branch, so
    rolling back means switching the Pages source back to it. Once the URL
    check passes against production, delete <code>mkdocs.yml</code>,{" "}
    <code>requirements.txt</code>, and <code>includes/</code>, and submit the
    new <code>sitemap.xml</code> in Google Search Console.
  </p>

  <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
  <h3>A page fails with "Could not parse expression"</h3>
  <p>
    An attribute list or a placeholder is still in an <code>.mdx</code> page:{" "}
    <code>{"{ .md-button }"}</code>, <code>{"{ width=\"300\" }"}</code>, or
    literal <code>{"{name}"}</code> text. MDX reads braces as JavaScript.
    Remove it, or escape the brace as <code>\{"{"}</code>. Some, like{" "}
    <code>{"{ width=\"300\" }"}</code>, compile and then fail while the page
    renders, which the build reports as <code>BLUME_MDX_UNDEFINED_NAME</code>{" "}
    at the line. <code>blume check</code> reports the others ahead of the
    build, with the file and line: <code>BLUME_MDX_SYNTAX</code>, and{" "}
    <code>BLUME_MDX_ATTRIBUTE_LIST</code> for an attribute list.
  </p>
  <h3>A page fails with "Expected a closing tag"</h3>
  <p>
    A placeholder in prose, like <code>uv run &lt;command&gt;</code>, reads as
    an HTML tag in MDX, and <code>blume check</code> reports it as{" "}
    <code>BLUME_MDX_SYNTAX</code> at its line. Put it in backticks.
  </p>
  <h3>Prettier turned a comment into a broken one</h3>
  <p>
    Prettier rewrites a multi-line <code>{"{/* … */}"}</code> comment into{" "}
    <code>{"{/_ … _/}"}</code>. Keep MDX comments on one line, as the codemod
    writes them.
  </p>
  <h3>The build fails on a page's frontmatter</h3>
  <p>
    <code>BLUME_FRONTMATTER_INVALID</code> with a message like{" "}
    <code>Unrecognized key: "categories"</code> means a Material key is left
    that the codemod reported. Map it or remove it.
  </p>
  <h3>An include fails the build</h3>
  <p>
    <code>BLUME_INCLUDE_OUTSIDE_ROOT</code> means the file sits outside{" "}
    <code>docs/</code>. Move it into <code>docs/_snippets/</code> and update
    the path. If the original keeps changing, copy it there in a{" "}
    <code>prebuild</code> script and ignore the copy in Git.
  </p>
</GuidePage>
